logs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use#breadcrumbs\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"BreadcrumbList\",\n      \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use#breadcrumbs\",\n      \"itemListElement\": [\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n          \"position\": 1,\n          \"name\": \"Home\",\n          \"item\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n          \"position\": 2,\n          \"name\": \"Blog\",\n          \"item\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"ListItem\",\n          \"position\": 3,\n          \"name\": \"Yes No Wheel When To Use\",\n          \"item\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"BlogPosting\",\n      \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use#article\",\n      \"headline\": \"The Yes or No Wheel: A Practical Guide to Breaking Decision Deadlock\",\n      \"description\": \"A Yes or No Wheel works by revealing your gut reaction, not by choosing for you. A practical, psychology-backed guide to when it helps, when it doesn't, and how to use it well.\",\n      \"image\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/eng-yes-no-wheel.webp\",\n      \"datePublished\": \"2026-10-15\",\n      \"dateModified\": \"2026-11-24\",\n      \"inLanguage\": \"en\",\n      \"author\": {\n        \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/#harman\"\n      },\n      \"publisher\": {\n        \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/#organization\"\n      },\n      \"mainEntityOfPage\": {\n        \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use#webpage\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n      \"@id\": \"https://spinthewheelsonline.com/blogs/yes-no-wheel-when-to-use#faq\",\n      \"mainEntity\": [\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Is the Yes or No Wheel actually 50/50?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Yes. Both halves share identical arc area and every spin pulls cryptographic entropy via window.crypto.getRandomValues() with rejection sampling—never fallback Math.random().\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"What if I feel nothing when it lands?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"A neutral reaction tells you either the stakes are genuinely low—so take the result and move forward—or you are missing information that needs quick pros-and-cons checking rather than a randomizer.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Can it help with decisions that have more than two options?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Yes or No is built for binary dilemmas. For three or more choices—such as restaurants, movies, or tasks—use a custom Picker Wheel or Decision Wheel with one slice per option.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Isn't using a random wheel to decide things kind of irrational?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Once facts are equal, spending another twenty minutes deliberating lowers focus for zero gain. Using a fast randomizer breaks analysis paralysis on low-stakes deadlocks.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"What if I keep wanting to spin it again?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"That sudden urge to re-spin reveals what your gut quietly preferred all along. Trust that signal, skip the extra spin, and act on the option you secretly hoped would win.\"\n          }\n        },\n        {\n          \"@type\": \"Question\",\n          \"name\": \"Do children respond to it the same way adults do?\",\n          \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n            \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n            \"text\": \"Often even better—teachers and parents use Yes/No wheels for quick binary choices because kids accept a shared, visual spin willingly without negotiating.\"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}"} />

