        <!-- ═══ SECTION 5 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="commitment">5. Using It as a Mirror, Not an Authority</h2>

        <p>
          The highest-value way to use a Yes or No Wheel isn't to hand a
          decision over to randomness — it's to use the act of spinning as a
          <strong>mirror</strong> that forces your real preference into view.
          This distinction changes how effective the tool is.
        </p>

        <h3>The way that doesn't work</h3>
        <p>
          Spin, check the result, think "let me try that again," and re-spin
          until the answer matches what you wanted. This is the most common
          misuse, and it actually confirms you already knew your answer before
          you ever spun. The wheel did its job — you just didn't listen. The
          urge to re-spin is itself the data point: you wanted the other option.
        </p>

        <h3>The way that does</h3>
        <p>
          Before spinning, say or write down a genuine commitment: "I'll act on
          this result." Then spin exactly once, pay attention to how you feel
          the instant it lands, and act on that feeling. A matching gut reaction
          means alignment — go with it. A pang of disappointment means you've
          just found your real answer — go the other way. The wheel's job is to
          reflect what's already there, not to decide anything on its own.
        </p>

        <div class="box box-amber">
          <div class="box-label">📋 A Simple Protocol</div>
          <p>
            For real decisions, this sequence gets the most out of a Yes or No
            Wheel:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the decision as one clear sentence before spinning</li>
            <li>Commit out loud: "I'll act on whatever this lands on"</li>
            <li>Spin once, and notice how you feel as it decelerates</li>
            <li>
              The instant it lands, register your immediate emotional response —
              before you start explaining it to yourself
            </li>
            <li>Result and gut reaction match? Proceed with the result.</li>
            <li>
              They don't match? You now know your real answer — go with that
              instead.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p>
          This isn't a party trick. It's the same principle a therapist uses
          when asking a client to sit with a hypothetical outcome — the response
          to an imagined result surfaces feelings that direct questioning often
          can't reach. The wheel creates the scenario. Your reaction is the
          actual diagnosis.
        </p>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 6 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="when-not">6. When to Leave the Wheel Alone</h2>

        <p>
          Any honest guide to a tool has to cover its limits. There are
          decisions where reaching for a random spinner isn't just unhelpful —
          it's a quiet way of avoiding thinking the situation genuinely
          requires. Here's when to skip it:
        </p>

        <ul class="uc-list">
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">⚠️</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Anything with major, irreversible consequences</strong>
              <span
                >Accepting a job, ending a relationship, a significant financial
                commitment, a medical decision — these carry asymmetric,
                hard-to-undo outcomes. A gut-reaction spin can be one input
                among many, but it can't substitute for careful thought,
                professional guidance, or a conversation with the people
                involved. If you're tempted to hand a decision this big to a
                wheel, that impulse itself is worth sitting with.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">⚠️</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Decisions where you're missing facts, not clarity</strong>
              <span
                >If your indecision comes from not having enough information —
                not from having all of it and still feeling uncertain — the
                wheel has nothing to offer. Go find the missing information
                first. The mechanism only works on emotional loops, not
                informational gaps.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">⚠️</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Decisions that involve someone else's stake</strong>
              <span
                >If the outcome materially affects a partner, colleague, or
                child, that person belongs in the conversation. Using a wheel to
                skip a talk that needs to happen isn't a shortcut — it removes
                someone's legitimate voice from a decision that's partly
                theirs.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">⚠️</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>When you already know the answer</strong>
              <span
                >Sometimes what looks like indecision is really reluctance — you
                know exactly what you should do, you just don't want to do it.
                No wheel changes that. The decision is already made; what's
                missing is the will to act on it. Spinning here is
                procrastination wearing a deliberation costume.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">⚠️</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Compulsive, anxious re-spinning</strong>
              <span
                >If you notice yourself spinning repeatedly, unwilling to accept
                any result other than the one you're hoping for, take that as a
                signal. It often means the underlying decision carries more
                anxiety than a spinner alone can resolve. Decisions in this
                category tend to benefit more from talking to someone you trust
                than from any random-selection tool.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 7: MISCONCEPTIONS (new) ═══ -->
        <h2 id="misconceptions">7. Common Misconceptions, Cleared Up</h2>

        <p>
          A surprising amount of skepticism about Yes or No Wheels comes from a
          few misunderstandings about what the tool is actually claiming to do.
          Worth clearing these up directly:
        </p>

        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>
            <strong>"It's just letting chance run your life."</strong> Not quite
            — chance only supplies the trigger. The actual decision comes from
            your own reaction to the outcome, which is a very different
            mechanism than "whatever the wheel says, goes."
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"If it's random, using it must be irrational."</strong>
            Deliberating past the point of new information arriving is its own
            kind of irrationality — it wastes time and produces decision fatigue
            without improving the decision. A well-placed random trigger can be
            the more rational move.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"People who use it can't make up their own minds."</strong>
            The opposite is usually true — using the wheel deliberately, with
            the protocol in Section 5, requires more self-awareness than either
            impulsively deciding or endlessly stalling.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"It only works for trivial stuff."</strong> It works best on
            decisions with emotional rather than informational stakes — which
            includes plenty of things that don't feel trivial at all, like
            whether to have a hard conversation or take a small leap.
          </li>
        </ul>

