        <!-- ═══ CONCLUSION ═══ -->
        <h2 id="conclusion">11. Conclusion</h2>

        <p>
          A Yes or No Wheel looks like a toy, and in the wrong context it can be
          used like one. But underneath the simple red-and-green half-wheel is a
          real mechanism worth taking seriously: our emotional system often has
          a clearer read on our actual preferences than our analytical mind
          does, and the fastest way to access that clarity is to give it a
          concrete outcome to react to rather than an open question to keep
          circling.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's not mysticism — it's simply how anticipatory emotion works.
          Watching a specific result approach at decreasing speed produces a
          genuine reaction, and that reaction is data. Whether the wheel lands
          on Yes or No matters less than how you feel when it does. The spin is
          the diagnostic; the landing is just the prompt. Used this way, a Yes
          or No Wheel is a surprisingly effective tool for a specific and very
          common problem: the stuck binary decision that isn't stuck because you
          lack information, but because your gut and your analysis haven't
          actually compared notes yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          For decisions that genuinely deserve more thought, more conversation,
          or more research — let the wheel wait. But for the daily accumulation
          of small loops that quietly drain attention? Spin it, notice how you
          feel, and move on. The decision was probably already made before you
          opened the browser tab. The wheel just helped you find it.
        </p>

